19:T60b,## The is-a relationship

**Inheritance** models an **is-a** relationship: a `Motorcycle` **is a** `Vehicle`, a `Car` **is a** `Vehicle`. You write the shared idea once in a **base class** and let the specific types extend it.

The payoff is **code reuse**. Put the common fields and methods in the parent, and every child gets them **automatically**, no copy-paste:

```
class Vehicle {
    protected int speed;
    void start() { ... }    // shared once
    void stop()  { ... }
}

class Car extends Vehicle {  // gets start/stop/speed for free
    void openTrunk() { ... } // and ADDS its own
}
```

## Override to specialise

A child can keep an inherited method as-is, or **override** it to change the behaviour. A `Motorcycle` might override `start()` to kick-start instead of turning a key. It can even call the parent's version first with `super`:

- **`super.start()`** runs the inherited logic, then the child adds its own steps, extending, not just replacing.

## Single vs multiple inheritance

Languages differ on how many parents a class may have. **Java** allows **single** class inheritance (one `extends`) but **multiple interface** inheritance (many `implements`). C++ allows multiple class inheritance, at the cost of the "diamond problem".

> Caution: reach for inheritance only for a **true, stable is-a**. If you just want to **reuse behaviour**, prefer **composition over inheritance**, hold a collaborator instead of extending it. A deep, leaky class hierarchy is far harder to change than a few small parts wired together.c:["$","div",null,{"className":"flex min-h-screen","children":[["$","$L17",null,{"activeModule":"lld-foundations","activeLesson":"inheritance"}],["$","$L18","inheritance",{"lesson":{"id":"inheritance","patternId":"lld-foundations","title":"Inheritance","kind":"concept","difficulty":"Intro","statement":"A subclass IS-A superclass, it inherits the parent’s fields and methods for free, and may add to or override them.","companies":["Amazon","Microsoft","Oracle"],"theory":"$19","approaches":[{"label":"A family tree","code":["class Vehicle {","    protected int speed;","    void start() { ... }","    void stop()  { ... }","}","","class Car        extends Vehicle { void openTrunk(){...} }","class Truck      extends Vehicle { void load(){...} }","class Motorcycle extends Vehicle { void start(){...} }  // override"],"steps":[{"view":"class","codeLine":1,"narration":"The base class holds what every vehicle shares: a protected speed field (#) and the start()/stop() operations. Define it once, here.","classDiagram":{"classes":[{"id":"veh","name":"Vehicle","fields":["# speed: int"],"methods":["+ start()","+ stop()"],"x":1,"y":0.2,"tint":"accent"},{"id":"car","name":"Car","methods":["+ openTrunk()"],"x":0,"y":1.7},{"id":"bike","name":"Motorcycle","methods":["+ start()"],"x":1,"y":1.7},{"id":"truck","name":"Truck","methods":["+ load()"],"x":2,"y":1.7}],"relations":[{"id":"rc","from":"car","to":"veh","kind":"inherit","label":"is-a"},{"id":"rb","from":"bike","to":"veh","kind":"inherit","label":"is-a"},{"id":"rt","from":"truck","to":"veh","kind":"inherit","label":"is-a"}],"shownClassIds":["veh"],"shownRelIds":[],"activeIds":["veh"]},"vars":[["# protected","shared with children"]]},{"view":"class","codeLine":7,"narration":"A solid arrow with a hollow triangle is INHERITANCE, “is-a”. Car extends Vehicle, so it INHERITS start/stop/speed for free, and ADDS openTrunk().","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:relations","shownClassIds":["veh","car"],"shownRelIds":["rc"],"activeIds":["car"]},"vars":[["▷ solid","inherits (is-a)"]]},{"view":"class","codeLine":8,"narration":"Truck is-a Vehicle too, same inheritance. It gets start/stop/speed automatically and adds its own load() on top.","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:relations","shownClassIds":["veh","car","truck"],"shownRelIds":["rc","rt"],"activeIds":["truck"]}},{"view":"class","codeLine":9,"narration":"Motorcycle also extends Vehicle, but it OVERRIDES start(), a kick-start instead of a key, while still inheriting stop() and speed unchanged.","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:relations","shownClassIds":["veh","car","truck","bike"],"shownRelIds":["rc","rt","rb"],"activeIds":["bike"]},"vars":[["override","specialise one method"]]},{"view":"class","narration":"Define once in the parent, specialise in the children. The shared code lives in a single place, change start() there and every vehicle that didn’t override it updates.","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:relations","shownClassIds":["veh","car","truck","bike"],"shownRelIds":["rc","rt","rb"],"activeIds":[]},"vars":[["reuse","shared code, one home"]]}]},{"label":"Override + super","code":["class Motorcycle extends Vehicle {","    void start() {","        super.start();   // reuse the parent’s logic","        kickEngine();    // then add my own step","    }","}"],"steps":[{"view":"sequence","codeLine":2,"narration":"A Client calls start() on a Motorcycle reference. Because the child OVERRODE start(), the overridden version runs, not the parent’s.","sequence":{"actors":[{"id":"c","label":"Client","kind":"actor"},{"id":"m","label":"Motorcycle"}],"messages":[{"id":"s1","from":"c","to":"m","label":"start()","kind":"call"},{"id":"s2","from":"m","to":"m","label":"super.start()","kind":"self"},{"id":"s3","from":"m","to":"m","label":"+ kick engine","kind":"self"},{"id":"s4","from":"m","to":"c","label":"running","kind":"return"}],"shownCount":1,"activeIndex":0},"vars":[["call","start()"],["runs","overridden version"]]},{"view":"sequence","codeLine":3,"narration":"The override doesn’t have to start from scratch: super.start() reuses the inherited logic, then “+ kick engine” adds the Motorcycle’s own step.","sequence":{"actors":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:sequence:actors","messages":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:sequence:messages","shownCount":3,"activeIndex":2},"vars":[["super.start()","reuse parent"],["+ kick","add child step"]]},{"view":"sequence","codeLine":5,"narration":"It returns running. Override + super lets a child EXTEND inherited behaviour, not merely replace it, keep what the parent did, then add more.","sequence":{"actors":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:sequence:actors","messages":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:sequence:messages","shownCount":4,"activeIndex":3},"vars":[["extend","not just replace"]]}]}]}}]]}]
